        "description": "<div id=\"remove_no_follow\">\n\t\t<div class=\"grid grid--cols-10@md grid--cols-8@lg article-column\">\n\t\t\t\t\t  <div class=\"col-12 col-10@md col-6@lg col-start-3@lg\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"article-column__content\">\n<section class=\"wp-block-bigbite-multi-title\"><div class=\"container\"></div></section>\n\n\n\n<p>The first full beta of Python 3.15 has arrived, and it\u2019s one of the most feature-packed Python releases in many a moon. Here\u2019s a rundown of the biggest, boldest, and most important innovations, changes, and fixes.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\" id=\"lazy-imports\">Lazy imports</h2>\n\n\n\n<p>A long-asked for feature, <a href=\"https://www.infoworld.com/article/4145854/speed-boost-your-python-programs-with-new-lazy-imports.html\">lazy imports</a> allow imports to be processed only when they\u2019re actually used by the program. Thus for slow-importing modules that impose a large cost on a program\u2019s startup time, you can now easily defer that cost to when the code of that module will actually be executed.",
